  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` and created `tests/test_money.py`, since there were no money tests before. The suite passes: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` ran 10 tests, all OK. Nothing is committed.\n\n```python\ndef split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]:\n    if parts < 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"parts must be >= 1: {parts}\")\n    base, extra = divmod(cents, parts)\n    return [base + 1 if i < extra else base for i in range(parts)]\n```\n\nThe new tests cover:\n- an even split\n- the remainder going to the first elements (e.g. `split(10, 3) == [4, 3, 3]`)\n- the result always summing to `cents`\n- `ValueError` for `parts` of 0 and -1\n\n**Decision for you:** the spec doesn't say what to do with negative `cents`, so I added no special handling. Right now `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`. That still sums to -10, but the largest debit lands on the *last* element rather than the first. If negative amounts can happen in your code, it's worth choosing a rule: reject them, or spread them from the first element the same way positive remainders are.",
